# Command / Query Responsibility Segregation (CQRS) in C#

> **TL;DR** – CQRS splits an application into *two* separate parts:
> * a **write model** (commands) that updates state,
> * a **read model** (queries) that reads state.

The two parts use different data structures and can evolve independently, giving you better scalability, clearer intent and, in many cases, performance benefits.

Below is a **complete, runnable example** written in **C# (.NET 7)** that demonstrates the pattern in its simplest form.  
After the code you’ll find a quick‑look explanation of *why* we do it and *what* the main pieces are.

---

## 1. The Idea

| **Side** | **What it does** | **Typical concerns** |
|----------|------------------|----------------------|
| **Command** | *“Do something.”* | Business rules, validation, persistence. |
| **Query** | *“Give me something.”* | Read‑only data, projection, caching. |

Both sides can be modeled as separate **interfaces** and can target different data stores (SQL vs. NoSQL, OLTP vs. OLAP, etc.).  
In many systems the write side ends up raising **domain events** that the read side uses to keep its projection in sync – this is the **event‑driven** part of CQRS.

---

## 2. Project Layout

```
src/
 └─ TodoApp/
     ├─ Domain/
     │   ├─ Entities/
     │   │   └─ Todo.cs
     │   ├─ Commands/
     │   │   ├─ AddTodoCommand.cs
     │   │   └─ AddTodoCommandHandler.cs
     │   ├─ Queries/
     │   │   ├─ GetTodosQuery.cs
     │   │   └─ GetTodosQueryHandler.cs
     │   └─ Events/
     │       └─ TodoAddedEvent.cs
     ├─ Application/
     │   ├─ Contracts/
     │   │   ├─ ICommand.cs
     │   │   ├─ IQuery.cs
     │   │   ├─ ICommandHandler.cs
     │   │   ├─ IQueryHandler.cs
     │   │   └─ IEventBus.cs
     │   └─ Mediator/  (wrapper around MediatR)
     ├─ Infrastructure/
     │   ├─ Persistence/
     │   │   └─ InMemoryDb.cs
     │   └─ EventBus/
     │       └─ SimpleEventBus.cs
     └─ Presentation/
         └─ Program.cs   (minimal API)
```

> **Tip:** In a real project you would split this into several projects (e.g., `Domain`, `Application`, `Infrastructure`, `Presentation`) and use a proper dependency‑injection container.  
> For brevity we keep everything in one file in the example below.

---

## 3. Code – One‑File Demo

> **Copy‑paste** the whole snippet into a new `Program.cs` file in a .NET 7 console or minimal‑API project and run.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Concurrent;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Builder;
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. Domain layer
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

namespace Domain.Entities
{
    public class Todo
    {
        public Guid Id { get; private set; }
        public string Title { get; private set; }
        public bool Done { get; private set; }

        public Todo(Guid id, string title)
        {
            Id = id;
            Title = title;
            Done = false;
        }

        public void MarkDone()
        {
            Done = true;
        }
    }
}

namespace Domain.Events
{
    public record TodoAddedEvent(Guid TodoId, string Title);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. Application contracts
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

namespace Application.Contracts
{
    // Marker interfaces
    public interface ICommand { }
    public interface IQuery<TResult> { }

    public interface ICommandHandler<TCommand> where TCommand : ICommand
    {
        Task HandleAsync(TCommand command, CancellationToken ct = default);
    }

    public interface IQueryHandler<TQuery, TResult> where TQuery : IQuery<TResult>
    {
        Task<TResult> HandleAsync(TQuery query, CancellationToken ct = default);
    }

    public interface IEventBus
    {
        Task PublishAsync<TEvent>(TEvent @event, CancellationToken ct = default);
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. In‑memory persistence (for demo only)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

namespace Infrastructure.Persistence
{
    // This is your write store (OLTP)
    public class TodoWriteStore
    {
        private readonly ConcurrentDictionary<Guid, Domain.Entities.Todo> _store
            = new();

        public void Add(Domain.Entities.Todo todo) => _store[todo.Id] = todo;

        public Domain.Entities.Todo Get(Guid id) => _store.TryGetValue(id, out var t) ? t : null!;
    }

    // This is your read store (OLAP/Projection)
    public class TodoReadStore
    {
        // Projection: id -> { title, done }
        private readonly ConcurrentDictionary<Guid, (string Title, bool Done)> _store
            = new();

        public void AddProjection(Guid id, string title)
            => _store[id] = (title, false);

        public IEnumerable<(Guid Id, string Title, bool Done)> GetAll()
            => _store.Select(kvp => (kvp.Key, kvp.Value.Title, kvp.Value.Done));
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. Simple event bus
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

namespace Infrastructure.EventBus
{
    using Application.Contracts;

    public class SimpleEventBus : IEventBus
    {
        private readonly IServiceProvider _provider;

        public SimpleEventBus(IServiceProvider provider) => _provider = provider;

        public async Task PublishAsync<TEvent>(TEvent @event, CancellationToken ct = default)
        {
            // Resolve all handlers for the event type
            var handlers = _provider.GetServices<IEventHandler<TEvent>>();
            foreach (var h in handlers)
            {
                await h.HandleAsync(@event, ct);
            }
        }
    }

    // Marker for event handlers
    public interface IEventHandler<TEvent>
    {
        Task HandleAsync(TEvent @event, CancellationToken ct = default);
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5. Commands & Handlers
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

namespace Domain.Commands
{
    using Application.Contracts;
    using Infrastructure.Persistence;
    using Domain.Events;
    using Infrastructure.EventBus;

    // 5.1 Command
    public record AddTodoCommand(string Title) : ICommand;

    // 5.2 Handler
    public class AddTodoCommandHandler : ICommandHandler<AddTodoCommand>
    {
        private readonly TodoWriteStore _writeStore;
        private readonly IEventBus _eventBus;

        public AddTodoCommandHandler(TodoWriteStore writeStore, IEventBus eventBus)
        {
            _writeStore = writeStore;
            _eventBus = eventBus;
        }

        public async Task HandleAsync(AddTodoCommand command, CancellationToken ct = default)
        {
            var todo = new Domain.Entities.Todo(Guid.NewGuid(), command.Title);
            _writeStore.Add(todo);

            // Publish domain event
            await _eventBus.PublishAsync(new TodoAddedEvent(todo.Id, todo.Title), ct);
        }
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 6. Queries & Handlers
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

namespace Domain.Queries
{
    using Application.Contracts;
    using Infrastructure.Persistence;

    // 6.1 Query
    public record GetTodosQuery() : IQuery<IEnumerable<TodoDto>>;

    // 6.2 DTO – read‑only view
    public record TodoDto(Guid Id, string Title, bool Done);

    // 6.3 Handler
    public class GetTodosQueryHandler : IQueryHandler<GetTodosQuery, IEnumerable<TodoDto>>
    {
        private readonly TodoReadStore _readStore;

        public GetTodosQueryHandler(TodoReadStore readStore) => _readStore = readStore;

        public Task<IEnumerable<TodoDto>> HandleAsync(GetTodosQuery query, CancellationToken ct = default)
        {
            var result = _readStore.GetAll()
                .Select(t => new TodoDto(t.Id, t.Title, t.Done));
            return Task.FromResult(result);
        }
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 7. Projection handler (event to read model)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

namespace Domain.EventHandlers
{
    using Application.Contracts;
    using Infrastructure.Persistence;
    using Domain.Events;

    public class TodoAddedEventHandler : IEventHandler<TodoAddedEvent>
    {
        private readonly TodoReadStore _readStore;

        public TodoAddedEventHandler(TodoReadStore readStore) => _readStore = readStore;

        public Task HandleAsync(TodoAddedEvent @event, CancellationToken ct = default)
        {
            _readStore.AddProjection(@event.TodoId, @event.Title);
            return Task.CompletedTask;
        }
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 8. Wire it all up with DI and a minimal API
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// 8.1 Register application services
builder.Services.AddSingleton<Infrastructure.Persistence.TodoWriteStore>();
builder.Services.AddSingleton<Infrastructure.Persistence.TodoReadStore>();
builder.Services.AddSingleton<Application.Contracts.IEventBus, Infrastructure.EventBus.SimpleEventBus>();

// 8.2 Register command/query/event handlers
builder.Services.AddTransient<Application.Contracts.ICommandHandler<Domain.Commands.AddTodoCommand>, Domain.Commands.AddTodoCommandHandler>();
builder.Services.AddTransient<Application.Contracts.IQueryHandler<Domain.Queries.GetTodosQuery, IEnumerable<Domain.Queries.TodoDto>>, Domain.Queries.GetTodosQueryHandler>();
builder.Services.AddTransient<Infrastructure.EventBus.IEventHandler<Domain.Events.TodoAddedEvent>, Domain.EventHandlers.TodoAddedEventHandler>();

var app = builder.Build();

// 8.3 Endpoints
app.MapPost("/todos", async (
        Domain.Commands.AddTodoCommand cmd,
        IServiceProvider sp,
        CancellationToken ct) =>
{
    var handler = sp.GetRequiredService<Application.Contracts.ICommandHandler<Domain.Commands.AddTodoCommand>>();
    await handler.HandleAsync(cmd, ct);
    return Results.Ok();
});

app.MapGet("/todos", async (
        Domain.Queries.GetTodosQuery query,
        IServiceProvider sp,
        CancellationToken ct) =>
{
    var handler = sp.GetRequiredService<Application.Contracts.IQueryHandler<Domain.Queries.GetTodosQuery, IEnumerable<Domain.Queries.TodoDto>>>();
    var todos = await handler.HandleAsync(query, ct);
    return Results.Ok(todos);
});

app.Run();
```

### What the demo shows

| Feature | Where it lives | How it works |
|---------|----------------|--------------|
| **Command** | `AddTodoCommand` + `AddTodoCommandHandler` | Handles a write request → mutates the write store → publishes `TodoAddedEvent`. |
| **Query** | `GetTodosQuery` + `GetTodosQueryHandler` | Reads the read store (projection). |
| **Event** | `TodoAddedEvent` | Fired by the command handler. |
| **Projection** | `TodoAddedEventHandler` → `TodoReadStore` | Keeps the read model in sync. |
| **DI** | `SimpleEventBus` + `MediatR`‑style registration | Decouples sender from receiver. |

Run the app (`dotnet run`) and try:

```bash
# Add a todo
curl -X POST http://localhost:5000/todos -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"title":"Buy milk"}'

# Read all todos
curl http://localhost:5000/todos
```

You’ll see the new todo in the list – the read model was automatically updated by the event handler.

---

## 4. Why CQRS?

| Reason | What you gain |
|--------|---------------|
| **Separation of concerns** | Commands only change state; queries only read. |
| **Independent scaling** | Read load can be distributed across cheap replicas; writes stay on a single source of truth. |
| **Optimised data models** | The read side can expose flattened projections tailored to the UI; the write side can keep a richer domain model. |
| **Flexibility for future growth** | Adding a new read view (e.g., “by priority”) just means a new projection, no changes to the write side. |
| **Better support for eventual consistency** | Commands can be processed quickly; read models can be updated asynchronously. |
| **Clearer business intent** | “AddTodo” is a *command* (intent) vs “GetTodos” is a *query* (information). |

**Potential trade‑offs**

| Issue | Mitigation |
|-------|------------|
| **More complexity** | Keep it simple; only split when you hit real scalability or read‑heavy workloads. |
| **Eventual consistency** | If you need strong consistency, run read queries against the write store (but you lose the CQRS benefit). |
| **Versioning of projections** | Store projection versions; rebuild if the schema changes. |
| **Tooling** | Use libraries like MediatR (for in‑process command/query dispatch) or MassTransit/NServiceBus (for distributed). |

---

## 5. Going further

* **Event‑Sourcing** – Store domain events instead of snapshots; read side builds projections from them.  
* **Message Bus** – Replace the simple in‑process `SimpleEventBus` with RabbitMQ, Kafka, Azure Service Bus, etc.  
* **CQRS with EF Core** – Use `DbContext` on the write side, read‑only `DbContext` or raw SQL for queries.  
* **Read‑Only cache** – Add a Redis layer for very hot queries.  
* **Automated projections** – Libraries like *Marten* or *EventStoreDB* automatically maintain read models.

---

## 6. TL;DR Cheat‑Sheet

| Term | Meaning | Typical C# Type |
|------|---------|----------------|
| **Command** | Intent to change state | `ICommand` (record) |
| **CommandHandler** | Executes a command | `ICommandHandler<TCommand>` |
| **Query** | Request for data | `IQuery<TResult>` |
| **QueryHandler** | Returns data | `IQueryHandler<TQuery, TResult>` |
| **Event** | Something that happened | POCO / record |
| **EventHandler** | Reacts to an event | `IEventHandler<TEvent>` |
| **Read Store / Projection** | Materialised read‑only view | `ConcurrentDictionary<…>` or DB table |
| **Write Store** | Source of truth for writes | `DbContext` or in‑memory store |
| **Event Bus** | Publishes events to handlers | `IEventBus` |

---

## 7. Closing Thoughts

CQRS is not a silver bullet.  
If you only have a few CRUD endpoints and low traffic, a monolithic “Command + Query” controller may be simpler.

However, when you:

* Have **heavy read traffic** (e.g., dashboards),
* Need **different data models** for reads vs. writes,
* Want to **scale** reads separately, or
* Intend to adopt **event‑driven architecture**,

CQRS is a powerful architectural pattern that keeps your code clean, maintainable, and scalable.  

Happy coding! 🚀
